sed on my  idea  as preemptive modesty uh this uh  indicates the balance between what we  armchair theorists do  and what the instrument builders and the  computer scientists do  so what's going to be the equivalent of  the hydroelectric dam do you think  well of course we already have it we  have a huge telescopes and uh  european scientists are building a  telescope in chile  which is um  called the extremely large telescope  they're rather unimaginative  the elt they're rather unimaginative in  the mencouture but it will have a mirror  um 39 meters across and that's i guess  probably a bit bigger than the width of  this this tent um and um that's going to  be uh wonderful new instruments we have  similar things in space which are  smaller and that's going to do a number  of things it's going to be able to probe  further away  and therefore further back in the past  so we understand the early history of  galaxies but also the other exciting  development in our subject has been the  realization that the stars we see in the  sky  are not just twinkling points of light  they are suns  mainly orbited by retinues of planets  just like the  sun is orbited by the earth and the  other familiar planets and these  so-called exoplanets  are  um  very hard to detect they're so faint  that we only know about them indirectly  but these  huge telescopes would be able to  actually image  a possible other earth  around another star and then of course  the next question is is there going to  be life on any of those and they're  going to be like many of these  yes  good question well i think the answer if  you don't know and um uh biology is a  much more complicated subject uh than uh  than physics or astronomy i like to say  that the smallest insect is harder to  understand and then a star or a galaxy  so we don't know yet but we will within  10 or 20 years know if there's some sort  of biosphere on some of these planets um  some evidence for life um intelligent  life of course um 